window.docContents[199].resolve({"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Next-Steps--Programming-with-Dependent-Types":{"contents":"For programmers who are interested in learning to use indexed families and dependent types to structure programs, Edwin Brady's Type Driven Development with Idris provides an excellent introduction.\nLike Rocq, Idris is a close cousin of Lean, though it lacks tactics.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Next Steps","header":"9.4. Programming with Dependent Types","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Next-Steps--Programming-with-Dependent-Types"},"/#additional-stipulations":{"contents":"In addition to adhering to the individual contracts associated with each type class, combined implementations ,  and  should work equivalently to these default implementations.\nIn other words, a type that provides both  and  instances should not have an implementation of  that works differently from the version that the  instance generates as a default implementation.\nThis is important because polymorphic functions may be refactored to replace a use of >>= with an equivalent use of <*>, or a use of <*> with an equivalent use of >>=.\nThis refactoring should not change the meaning of programs that use this code.\n\nThis rule explains why  should not be used to implement  in a  instance.\nOn its own, it obeys the monad contract.\nHowever, when it is used to implement , the behavior is not equivalent to  itself.\nTo see where they differ, take the example of two computations, both of which return errors.\nStart with an example of a case where two errors should be returned, one from validating a function (which could have just as well resulted from a prior argument to the function), and one from validating an argument:\n\n\n\nCombining them with the version of <*> from 's  instance results in both errors being reported to the user:\n\n\n\nUsing the version of  that was implemented with >>=, here rewritten to , results in only the first error being available:\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Functors, Applicative Functors, and Monads\u0009The Applicative Contract","header":"5.3.3. Additional Stipulations","id":"/#additional-stipulations"},"/#props-proofs-indexing-exercises":{"contents":"* Prove the following theorems using : , , . What happens if  is used to prove ? Why?* Prove the following theorems using : , , , .* Write a function that looks up the fifth entry in a list. Pass the evidence that this lookup is safe as an argument to the function.\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Interlude: Propositions, Proofs, and Indexing\u0009Messages You May Meet","header":"Exercises","id":"/#props-proofs-indexing-exercises"}});